Use dynamic programming: dp[i][j] means the first i string characters fully match the first j pattern characters. A star either skips its preceding atom or consumes another matching character.

```javascript
function solve(s, p) {
  const m = s.length;
  const n = p.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () =>
    Array(n + 1).fill(false)
  );
  dp[0][0] = true;

  for (let j = 2; j <= n; j++) {
    if (p[j - 1] === '*') dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 2];
  }

  for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
      if (p[j - 1] === '*') {
        const atom = p[j - 2];
        const matches = atom === '.' || atom === s[i - 1];
        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2] || (matches && dp[i - 1][j]);
      } else {
        const matches = p[j - 1] === '.' || p[j - 1] === s[i - 1];
        dp[i][j] = matches && dp[i - 1][j - 1];
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[m][n];
}
```

Call solve(s, p) directly with a valid pattern; it returns a boolean. Time and space: O((m + 1)(n + 1)), where m and n are the string and pattern lengths.

